---
// Lesson 11.1b — Create PT: Build Day 1
// hybrid CPT build day: the list + the data structure core.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>11.1b — Create PT: Build Day 1</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Build day one, PT rules: write the LIST-CENTERED core. The list
    is the requirement graders poke hardest — today it goes in
    first, works alone, and prints so you can SEE the data move
    (input, behavior, output, remember).
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The day's contract:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>The program RUNS — even if it only prints</li>
        <li>The list EXISTS and participates: add, remove, access,
            or traverse — not a decoration
        </li>
        <li>A <strong>Debug.Log(...) rendering</strong> of the list's
            state after each change, so "behavior" is visible
            NOW (graders see a VIDEO later; you see the console
            today)</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Starter-voice template for a list-centered core</strong>
    (adapt to your program — names matter for your WR answers):</p>
    <pre><code>private List&lt;string&gt; inventory = new List&lt;string&gt;();

public void AddItem(string itemName)      // procedure + parameter!
{
    if (!inventory.Contains(itemName))    // selection
    {
        inventory.Add(itemName);
        Debug.Log("added " + itemName + " → " + ListContents());
    }
}

private string ListContents()
{
    string s = "";
    foreach (string item in inventory)    // iteration
    {
        s += item + " | ";
    }
    return s;                             // output of a procedure
}</code></pre>
    <p>That one method pair already has: procedure, parameter,
    selection, iteration, list participation, observable output.
    Your <a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a> muscle, aimed at a
    rubric.</p>

    <p><strong>The WR2a pre-answer (5 min, write it NOW while
    coding):</strong> "The list ___ stores ___; the program appends
    when ___, removes when ___, and traverses to ___." Write the
    sentence the same day you write the code — future-you will
    not remember why the list exists; today-you does.</p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>


    <div class="socratic">


        <h3>Socratic: The List Goes In First</h3>


        <ol>


            <li>The list went in before anything that uses it. What did printing the list tell you that reading the code would not have?</li>


            <li>Your list is the requirement graders poke hardest. Which of your list operations could a grader point at as proof, and does your code show it clearly?</li>


            <li>Today the program only had to work alone, with no user. What did you postpone, and how do you know the list will still hold when the input arrives?</li>


        </ol>


    </div>



    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Run the program with the list visibly participating</li>
            <li>Point at a procedure with a parameter and its CALL site</li>
            <li>Show selection and iteration inside one algorithm</li>
            <li>Read your WR2a sentence out loud and nod</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html">Lists &amp; Arrays</a> — your core, documented</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">Create PT guide</a> — the rubric rows you are feeding</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.1c.html">11.1c — Create PT: Build Day 2</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.1b" title="11.1b Create PT: Build Day 1 — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
